message: "If you use this paper or its verification engines, please cite this work."
title: "How much dimension does an epsilon-approximation to a non-attained quantum correlation cost? A Diophantine answer for K_5"
type: article
authors:
  - given-names: Seth
    family-names: Douglas
    email: apsiape@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"
version: 1.1.0
date-released: "2026-11-11"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21876896
license: CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  Dykema, Paulsen and Prakash proved that the finite-dimensional quantum
  correlation set C_q(5,2) is not closed via a K_5 graph correlation function,
  by an argument existential in dimension. We answer the quantitative question
  it leaves open. Let D(eps) be the least block dimension of a
  finite-dimensional carrier whose value exceeds the infimum by at most eps, at
  exact marginals and at the distinguished parameter t = 1/sqrt(5). Then D(eps)
  = Theta(eps^(-1/4)), and the exponent is arithmetic in origin: the lower bound
  is the Diophantine inequality ||q sqrt(5)|| > 1/(5q) transported through the
  law of total variance, and the upper bound is attained by feeding consecutive
  continued-fraction convergents of sqrt(5) into a two-block carrier, where the
  deficit is exactly the product of the two one-sided approximation errors. In
  the denominator convention the minimum is exact, e_Q = (a_Q - sqrt5)(sqrt5 -
  b_Q) with Farey neighbours of order Q; the sharp liminf and limsup of
  D_den(eps) eps^(1/4) are 0.7434960689 and 1.5747499445, and D_den <= D <= 2
  D_den. The same supporting-line inequality determines the fixed-target drift
  threshold exactly, with liminf constant 0.3642394613. We also show that orbit
  topology and attainment are different invariants on this object, and contrast
  K_5 with I3322, where the analogous cost is only logarithmic, concluding that
  dimension laws for non-closure witnesses are governed by the arithmetic of the
  parameter rather than by a universal rate. Version 1.1.0 adds the exact Farey
  law, the sharp constants and the exact drift law, and corrects three v1.0.2
  sentences that were false as written: Sections 4.1 and 5.3 called the
  convergents the extremisers, and Section 5.2 called the convergent deficit
  ratio an exact fixed factor.
keywords:
  - sums of projections
  - tracial states
  - quantum correlations
  - non-closure
  - continued fractions
  - Diophantine approximation
  - Farey sequence
  - Coxeter functor
  - Tits form
  - star quiver
  - approximation rate
  - dimension law
  - exact arithmetic verification
